NAME
    login - the shell's authentication gate

SYNOPSIS
    (automatic - the shell prompts for login at boot and after each logout)

DESCRIPTION
    Before every session the shell prompts:

        login: <username>
        password: <not echoed>

    and checks the password against /etc/shadow. On success it becomes that
    user (uid/gid) for the session; the prompt is # for root, $ for anyone else.
    A wrong username or password prints "Login incorrect" and re-prompts.

    Type `logout` (or `exit`) to end a session: the shell restores root and
    shows the login prompt again, so a different user can log in. Each session
    starts clean (fresh environment, home directory), so nothing leaks between
    users.

    The shell itself never exits - it is task 0, the sole keyboard owner - so
    "logout" ends the session, it does not stop the machine (use shutdown/halt
    for that).

CREDENTIALS
    /etc/passwd (world-readable) holds one account per line:

        name:uid:gid:home

    and /etc/shadow (0600, root's) the password secret for each:

        name:<salt>:<hash>

    A version-2 line, which useradd and passwd write, has a salt field of
    "2$" and 14 hex digits (a 7-byte random salt) and the first 32 bytes of
    PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA-512(password, salt, 210000 iterations) as its hash: about
    a second to check on the QEMU guest, on purpose, so a stolen shadow file
    is slow to guess at. A version-1 line (16 hex digits of salt, the hash
    SHA-256(salt || password)) still verifies, and a successful login
    rewrites it as version 2 in place. An old /etc/passwd line with the
    secret inline still verifies too, and is not upgraded.

    The DEV images ship accounts root/root (uid 0), user/user (uid 1000) and
    guest/guest; a real deployment would set its own. If /etc/passwd is
    missing (a freshly formatted disk, say), login starts a single root
    session so the machine stays usable.

    A logged-in user starts in their home directory (the passwd `home` field,
    normally /Users/<name>); the shell exports it as HOME, so `~` expands to
    it. Manage accounts with useradd, passwd, groupadd and usermod.

CLUSTER KEY
    On a node with a cluster realm (/etc/cluster/realm), login also derives
    the user's cluster key from the password, the name and the realm (another
    second), and hands it to netd, which holds it until the session ends and
    signs that user's cluster requests with it. An export that lists the user
    in /etc/cluster/users serves them only with that signature, so a user is
    served there only while logged in somewhere. With no realm, nothing is
    derived and login is otherwise the same. A full key table (four logins)
    says "login: note: the cluster key is not held" and the login goes on.
    clusterkey held (root) lists whose keys are held.

